WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.900
MISHA BRUKMAN: Welcome
everyone to the session.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:06.941
My name is Misha Brukman.

00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:09.310
I'm the product manager
for Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.140
And with us today,
we have Leon Stein

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.810
from eBay, who will talk
about how eBay brings together

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:17.110
billions of product listings to
enable new shopping experiences

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.050
using Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.770
Leon--

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.900
LEON STEIN: Hi.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.770
So first, a few
facts about eBay.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:30.020
eBay is a truly global company.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.209
We have 167 million buyers
in over 200 countries

00:00:34.209 --> 00:00:35.000
all over the world.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:43.040
Over 57% of our business
is international.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.390
And we are pretty big on
mobile with close to half

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.920
of business coming from mobile
and 348 million mobile app

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.740
downloads.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:02.590
Other brands of eBay include
eBay Classifieds and StubHub.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:10.900
So at the heart of what
eBay does is eBay catalog.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:16.500
And eBay catalog is
pretty big and it's

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.860
very rich and very complex.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:25.060
So we have well over 1
billion listings available

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:29.960
worldwide in eBay catalog
at any given moment.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:35.380
And over 15 million listings are
added to the catalog every day.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:40.600
And catalog is comprised
of many different datasets,

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.520
coming from many
different data sources.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.830
So if you look at
an eBay listing,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:53.530
you will see obvious things
like Title, Price, Variance,

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.780
Description, Attributes, Images.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.860
But there are also many data
elements that are not visible.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.610
And they are computed
behind the scenes,

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:11.200
by many different algorithms
to enhance selling and buying

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.160
experience.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:18.970
So it's not unusual for an
eBay listing to take megabytes,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.340
not counting images.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.410
And of course the volume of
updates of eBay catalog is--

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.730
of various datasets in
eBay catalog is very high,

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.470
and we will talk more about it.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:40.330
So with our team, which is
New Product Development team,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:45.190
and we build applications
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.840
First thing we wanted to
bring over is the catalog.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:56.670
And we needed a
scalable platform

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:04.290
to effectively enable new
shopping experiences globally,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.650
showcasing eBay inventory.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:12.690
We wanted a platform that allows
us to scale on demand with data

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.360
volume and traffic.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.645
We wanted to be able to
iterate quickly and integrate

00:03:19.645 --> 00:03:22.752
with data-heavy AI technologies.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.730
Our first application that
we launched this last October

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.260
is eBay ShopBot.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:33.670
So you can go--

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:37.210
if you happen to have a
mobile device or a laptop--

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:42.311
you can go to shopbot.ebay.com
and start playing with it.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.140
So eBay ShopBot is a new--

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:56.090
is a newer, smarter, and easier
way to shop in chatbot context.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:01.910
And it leverages AI with NLU,
dialog management, and also

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:02.540
visual search.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.930
So you can snap a picture of
an item you're interested in

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.900
and submit it to ShopBot
and you will hopefully

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.290
find something very close.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:16.220
So there are a number of
challenges, of course,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.699
with bringing all that data
over to Google Cloud platform.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.720
First, the catalog
had to be brought

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.900
from many different sources.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.690
And we wanted to bring
it in a way that is

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.310
really convenient to work with.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.810
So that instead of having
different pieces of catalog

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:41.760
in different systems, we wanted
to have all data effectively

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.870
together, so it's
much easier to work

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:51.210
with it in different contexts
and enable different shopping

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.070
use cases.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:57.090
So we also needed
to provide a highly

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:03.650
scalable and consistently fast
way of accessing catalog data.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.770
We have hundreds of
thousands of updates

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:11.810
per second on
catalog data with is

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:18.050
hundreds of megabytes written,
and very high volume reads.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:22.920
For example, an update to
catalog results in search index

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:23.690
update.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.870
And in order to re-index
a listing in real time,

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.420
you need to know
more information

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.510
about the listing than
happened with just that update.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.680
So we have more reads in
terms of volume than writes.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:41.030
So our reads are in many
gigabytes per second.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.110
And of course we
wanted very low latency

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:51.519
replicating catalog data
from eBay core system

00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:52.560
to Google Cloud platform.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.460
So that it is important
in retail domain

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.320
to have up-to-date data.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.750
So if listing is no
longer available,

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.880
we don't want to show it.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:06.260
If price changes, we want
to show updated price.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:11.300
And we also needed very high
consistency of the data.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.920
So that we-- in case
if an update is missed

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.320
in near real time, we
still want to make sure

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.440
that we have a mechanisms
that repairs this data,

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.220
so the replicated
data is eventually

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:29.360
correct and consistent
with what we have in core.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:33.000
So for example, if price
changes or listing got bought

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.450
and we didn't catch
this event, we

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.130
want to have a
mechanism in place that

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.150
ensures that we will have
data eventually correct

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:41.690
in Google platform.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.630
So about Google Cloud platform--

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:53.530
it is-- there are
many great features

00:06:53.530 --> 00:07:00.340
and I just want to highlight a
few of them that we leverage.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.280
It is first-- it
is global presence,

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:07.960
which eBay as a global company,
needs a platform that allows--

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.930
that supports global
presence really well.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:14.080
Also comprehensive and highly
coherent technology stack.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.780
And-- we are cutting edge.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.765
We like to work with
newest technologies

00:07:19.765 --> 00:07:27.730
and GCP provides those
new technologies for us.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:31.880
And it has leading
support for Kubernetes

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:32.830
with Container Engine.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:39.080
And we leverage Kubernetes
in many of our services,

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.740
so it was very important to us.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.360
And finally, there are some
unique offerings in GCP.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.910
There are many of
them, but for this talk

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.310
I want to focus
on Bigtable, that

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:57.130
was the data storage that
we've selected for the catalog.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:01.260
And we will go in more detail
on how we use Bigtable.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:07.180
With that-- so this is
a high-level diagram

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.940
that just covers our
data injection pipeline.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.930
It doesn't cover our whole
infrastructure on GCP.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:15.800
It's much bigger,
but this is just

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.870
the part that is responsible
for getting that catalog

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.720
from eBay core systems to GCP.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.700
And in the next
slides, we will go

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:27.640
in more detail in parts of it.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.890
And with that, I'll
pass it back to Misha

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:39.555
for a deeper dive in
GCP and the systems

00:08:39.555 --> 00:08:41.929
MISHA BRUKMAN: Before we talk
about Google Cloud Platform

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:44.059
in more detail, I
thought it would

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:46.790
be worthwhile to look at
the history of Google,

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:49.700
and where Google comes from,
and how we arrived to the point

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:52.080
that we are today.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.900
Google originally set out
to organize the world's

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.490
information and
make it universally

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.080
accessible and useful.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.130
And for that, Google
built web search.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.020
Now, that is a very
specific application.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.630
There is a lot of very
search-specific algorithms

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:08.940
and details that go
on behind the scenes.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.890
But even with this very
specific application,

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:13.590
Google had to solve a
number of very generic,

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:17.130
standard, big data problems,
to be able to enable the search

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.080
application service.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.560
For one, we had to be able to
store a large amount of data.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.990
And depending on the type
and the content of the data,

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.406
we need to use a
different storage

00:09:26.406 --> 00:09:28.840
system for those
different types of data.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.920
So we needed to use
file systems, databases,

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.417
and other types of
storage systems.

00:09:34.417 --> 00:09:36.000
Once we had that
data, we needed to be

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.250
able to process it at scale.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.920
And we needed to both be able
to process that data in batch

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.610
mode, to analyze the link
structure of the worldwide web,

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.650
as well to update data in real
time as pull up pages changed.

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:50.960
So we needed a real-time
streaming pipeline as well.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.580
And then to be able
to serve the data,

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.880
we needed to be able to deploy
and update our services,

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:57.734
and we use a micro
service architecture.

00:09:57.734 --> 00:10:00.150
We needed to update them quickly
and without any downtime.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:07.020
Google built a number of generic
infrastructure components.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.620
And with that, it enabled
a number of teams,

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:11.840
across many different
domains within Google,

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.050
to build a number of
services that each of which

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.760
serve over a billion users.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.490
So each of the services that
you see featured on this slide,

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:23.060
from Google itself, Google.com,
to Google Play, Chrome,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.600
Android, YouTube, Maps, Gmail,
and Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.130
Each of those serve
over a billion users.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.010
And the way that we've
been able to do that

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:34.970
is by using similar
architecture and infrastructure

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:36.960
components across all of them.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.830
So all of these services
benefit from the same hardware

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.950
infrastructure, networking,
the global network

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.010
that we have built, to enable
them to operate at the scale

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:47.510
that they do.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:51.110
Not only the hardware,
but also the software.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:53.840
Over the years, Google has built
a number of software components

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.450
to enable all those
projects to scale.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.820
Starting with file
systems such as GFS,

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.790
on to processing systems
such as MapReduce, Flume,

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:06.140
and MillWheel, to analytic
systems like Dremel,

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:08.960
databases like
Spanner and Bigtable,

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.557
and container
managers like Borg.

00:11:10.557 --> 00:11:12.890
Google has been able to build
these generic systems such

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:15.552
that each of those teams were
able to innovate and build

00:11:15.552 --> 00:11:17.510
the domain-specific
components that they needed

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:20.527
for their services without
having to reinvent and resolve

00:11:20.527 --> 00:11:22.610
the standard big data
problems that they would all

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.697
face consistently.

00:11:24.697 --> 00:11:26.780
And now with all of these
technologies that Google

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:28.430
has built over the
years to support

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:30.740
all of its own products,
we're making all of them

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:33.860
available on Google Cloud
Platform in the various areas

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:37.550
of Compute, Storage,
Databases, Big Data Processing,

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:38.780
and Machine Learning.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.030
So that anyone can use
these building blocks

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:43.550
to build their own applications
and their own services

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:46.070
and have them seamlessly
scale to serve

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:48.380
millions and billions of users.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:51.810
Let's talk about a couple of
these components in more depth.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:53.480
So for one, as I
mentioned, we needed

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.490
to be able to run systems.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:58.640
And we needed to upgrade
these systems very quickly

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.420
and efficiently
with no downtime.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.580
Google started off by
building container support

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:05.440
by writing the cgroup
support, that we

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.590
open sourced and contributed
to Linux, over a decade ago.

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:10.210
But we also needed
to be able to run

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:12.010
these containers as workloads.

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:14.710
So we built Borg
and then we open

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:18.250
sourced a container
manager called Kubernetes.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:20.980
And now we've created a
managed service offering

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.460
that uses Kubernetes-- and this
is Google Container Engine.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:27.280
Container Engine provides
cluster management

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.650
and orchestration for your
container as workloads.

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.170
It also provides a private
container registry for you

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.210
to deploy your applications.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.250
Applications can use a
declarative specification

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:38.470
for resources that
Kubernetes and Container

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.335
Engine will enforce.

00:12:40.335 --> 00:12:42.460
And it also provides
auto-scaling to meet the needs

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:45.530
of growing applications.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:47.260
We also needed to have
a file system that

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:50.190
would scale to the growing
needs of the internet.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.000
And so we started off was--

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.340
we built Google File
System, and then we've

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:56.980
upgraded that with Colossus.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:58.690
And today we offer
that as a service,

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.590
as Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:04.510
Google Cloud Storage is an
exabyte-scale storage system.

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:05.920
It provides high-throughput.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:08.680
It provides replicated
storage across regional and

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:09.864
multi-regional modes.

00:13:09.864 --> 00:13:11.530
It also provides
several different types

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.330
of classes for your
data, depending

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:16.930
on whether your data is hot or
cold, or somewhere in-between.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.600
And it uses the same API, so you
don't have to change your code,

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.720
depending on which type
of data you're accessing.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:25.377
It also provides an
HDFS-compatible connector

00:13:25.377 --> 00:13:27.710
for Hadoop, so you don't have
to change your code if you

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:28.834
are using Hadoop with HDFS.

00:13:28.834 --> 00:13:32.110
Two It will work seamlessly
with Google Cloud Storage, again

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.136
across all those
different storage classes

00:13:34.136 --> 00:13:35.260
and different bucket types.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:39.270
Once we have the
data, we need to be

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:41.400
able to process that at scale.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.977
And we're talking about both
static and streaming data.

00:13:45.977 --> 00:13:47.810
Google has written
several different systems

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:50.180
from batch to streaming,
such as MapReduce, Flume,

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:51.410
and MillWheel.

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.450
And then we combined
all of those together,

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.780
to offer as a single product,
Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:58.705
Then we went a step
further, and we've open

00:13:58.705 --> 00:14:02.690
sourced the SDK behind Google
Cloud Dataflow as Apache Beam.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:04.820
Apache Beam recently
became a top level project

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.450
in the Apache
Software Foundation.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:10.050
Dataflow combines both
batch and streaming modes.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.550
So you don't have to change
your code from one to the other.

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:14.440
You can write your
code once, and you

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:17.850
can run the same code in both
batch and streaming modes.

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.860
Dataflow provides
auto-scaling and dynamic task

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:24.160
sharding so that you're not
affected by long stragglers

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.640
doubling or tripling the
total length of your job.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.980
As Dataflow notices
that some of the workers

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.810
are struggling to
process the data,

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:33.829
it will dynamically
split those data

00:14:33.829 --> 00:14:35.620
and then autoscale and
provide more workers

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:39.070
to handle those shards that
are taking longer than others.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.680
Dataflow provides connectors
to our various storage systems,

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.400
such as BigQuery Bigtable,
and Cloud Storage,

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.420
so you can read and
write data from anywhere.

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:49.095
And as I mentioned, code that
uses the open source Apache

00:14:49.095 --> 00:14:52.220
Beam SDK also becomes portable.

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.270
You can run Apache Beam
workloads on Dataflow,

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.829
as well as Spark and Flink.

00:14:58.829 --> 00:15:01.120
We also wanted to be able to
run open source processing

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:04.540
engines, such as Hadoop
and Spark, directly.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:07.740
So for that, we built
Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:11.260
Dataproc allows you to run
could Hadoop, Spark, Pig, Hive,

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.580
and other open source
big data systems.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:16.009
It also provides connectors
to Bigtable, BigQuery,

00:15:16.009 --> 00:15:17.050
and Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.540
So again, you can read
and write from anywhere.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.794
And because it can read and
write data from anywhere,

00:15:21.794 --> 00:15:23.710
you don't have to write
in stateless clusters.

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:26.080
Instead, you can run
stateless clusters

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:27.889
that have their storage
external to them.

00:15:27.889 --> 00:15:29.680
Which means you can
very quickly and easily

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:32.290
spin-up clusters, run
the jobs that you need,

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:33.730
and spin them down.

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:35.980
Dataproc also provides
a higher level jobs

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:38.440
API, such that you
can just submit jobs,

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:40.960
rather than thinking
about clusters--

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:42.920
long-running clusters
managing those clusters.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:50.130
We also needed to have a
petabyte-scale database.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:54.097
When we used to build our search
indexes in batch mode in bulk,

00:15:54.097 --> 00:15:56.430
it took a long time to keep
rebuilding the entire search

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:57.540
index.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.769
It also made the index
grow stale over time

00:15:59.769 --> 00:16:01.310
because we would
not be able to catch

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:03.990
the really quickly changing
web pages as they changed

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:05.170
in real time.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:08.190
So what we needed was a
real-time, low latency,

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:12.030
access to data sets
scaled into the petabytes.

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.782
And for that, we wrote Bigtable.

00:16:14.782 --> 00:16:16.240
And we're now
offering of Bigtable,

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.823
as Google Cloud Bigtable, as a
fully managed service on Google

00:16:18.823 --> 00:16:20.146
Cloud platform.

00:16:20.146 --> 00:16:23.050
Bigtable is a fully
managed NoSQL database.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.900
As I mentioned, it provides
petabyte scale data scale

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:30.130
storage with low latency access
for both reads and writes.

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.920
It provides very seamless
scalability for throughput

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:35.740
and it learns and adjusts
to access patterns.

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:38.080
Let's look into more detail
about those last two points

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.570
and how we provide that.

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:42.790
So first let's talk
about the structure

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:44.770
and the schema for Bigtable.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:49.690
Bigtable is a NoSQL, sparse,
wide-column database.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:53.710
So what that means is that
Bigtable has a single key,

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:55.360
which is the row key.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:59.274
It does not do joins and it only
does single row transactions.

00:16:59.274 --> 00:17:00.940
It's sparse, so that
means that whenever

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:03.220
you don't store the data
in any one of the cells,

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:05.500
it does not count
against the storage.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.569
It is wide-column,
which means that you

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:09.010
can create column families.

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.540
And within each
column family, you

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:13.270
can create, essentially, an
unlimited number of columns.

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:15.700
So you can store any
number of data items, keyed

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:19.920
to a single row, as you need.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:22.170
The way to think about of
Bigtable, as a data model,

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.119
is as a three-dimensional
spreadsheet.

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:25.050
There are rows.

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:25.950
There are columns.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:27.950
And each one of the cell
has an arbitrary number

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:30.840
of versions which are keyed
with a 64-bit integer.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.742
You can treat that
64-bit integer as time,

00:17:32.742 --> 00:17:34.200
and that is a very
common use case.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.200
But you could also treat that
as just an arbitrary version

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:39.250
and just assign them
versions as 1, 2, and 3.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:45.570
Bigtable is an unopinionated
storage system.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:48.100
So you can store
any type of data.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.820
It's uninterpreted so
they're just bytes.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.650
And there are many
different applications

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.480
that can be built
on top of Bigtable.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:55.938
However, for each
one of those, you

00:17:55.938 --> 00:17:59.970
would have to design
custom row key schemas.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.910
For a number of
standard use cases,

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:05.804
there are open source systems
that already predefine

00:18:05.804 --> 00:18:08.220
those schemas such that you
can implement these common use

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.097
cases very easily.

00:18:10.097 --> 00:18:12.180
So Bigtable has need of
support for a time series,

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.037
because it has that
third dimension of time.

00:18:14.037 --> 00:18:15.870
But you would still
have to carefully design

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:20.190
your time series schema to make
sure that it performs well.

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:22.530
Or you can use one of
the open source systems,

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.740
such as Heroic by
Spotify, or OpenTSTB,

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.900
which is an open source
time series database.

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.890
In fact, Spotify runs their
monitoring system today

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:35.410
on Bigtable using
the Heroic system.

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:37.180
Alternatively, if
you need to run

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:40.810
graph workloads-- of course, you
can implement a graph schema,

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.240
using something like
adjacency lists,

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:46.010
using the wide-column database.

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:49.870
You can also use one of these
two graph abstraction systems,

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:53.500
such as HgraphDb or
JanusGraph, to provide you

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:56.680
with a standard, tinker pop
API implementation on top

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:58.470
of Bigtable.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.930
JanusGraph is something that
we just launched recently,

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:03.810
in cooperation with several
other companies, IBM,

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:06.540
Hortonworks, Expero and GRAKN.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.960
This is a new project,
as of two months ago,

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:12.510
in the Linux Foundation that
is now working with Bigtable.

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:16.240
It also supports other open
source wide-column databases.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.070
Let's talk about the
Bigtable implementation

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.090
and the architecture behind it.

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:25.910
So this is a somewhat
simplified architecture,

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:29.290
and we're going to go into
depth in the next few slides.

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:33.070
Clients connect to a load
balancing proxy endpoint which

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:37.666
then connect to the processing
nodes of Bigtable server nodes.

00:19:37.666 --> 00:19:39.040
In the next few
slides, I'm going

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:42.220
to remove the load balancing
proxy layer, for clarity,

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:45.130
to show where the requests
are actually routed.

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:47.260
But just remember that
there is a single endpoint,

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:48.730
so there is no need
for a discovery

00:19:48.730 --> 00:19:51.640
service for each client to
know where to connect to.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.610
All the clients connect
to a single endpoint.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:58.170
One interesting
thing about Bigtable

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:01.990
is that as a database, Bigtable
itself is actually stateless.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:04.330
Bigtable does not
store any data.

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:06.780
And this is actually true
for all the other big Google

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.160
storage systems.

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:11.930
Bigtable, Spanner, BigQuery,
and Google Cloud Storage

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:15.340
all use the Colossus
file system.

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:17.990
So Bigtable nodes
provide you with access,

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:20.600
and they mediate the
requests between the clients

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:21.230
and the data.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:22.604
But the Bigtable
nodes themselves

00:20:22.604 --> 00:20:23.840
don't store any data.

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:26.870
Now this provides a couple
interesting features

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:30.159
as to how Bigtable operates.

00:20:30.159 --> 00:20:31.700
Let's say we are
looking at an access

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:35.360
pattern such as the one
in the diagram, where

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.090
the Bigtable node on
the left is getting more

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:39.380
requests than the other nodes.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:42.650
And that is because it is
managing the data in Colossus,

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:45.740
the regions A, B, and
C, and that data appears

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:47.960
to be popular with the clients.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:50.420
As Bigtable noticed
that one of the nodes

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:52.720
is getting more
activity than others,

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:56.150
it can easily reassign
the ownership of that data

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:57.890
to a different node.

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:00.140
And the request would be
rerouted automatically.

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.345
Again, remember there is that
load balancing proxy layer.

00:21:03.345 --> 00:21:05.720
This is possible to do quickly
because we're not actually

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:07.490
moving any of the data.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:09.380
None of the nodes
actually store the data

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:12.004
within their own containers.

00:21:12.004 --> 00:21:13.670
So this is just a
metadata reassignment,

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:15.044
which happens very quickly.

00:21:15.044 --> 00:21:17.210
We don't need to copy
terabytes or petabytes of data

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:18.710
to be able to do
this re-assignment,

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:20.070
so this happens very quickly.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:22.255
In fact, this
happens all the time.

00:21:22.255 --> 00:21:24.380
So for example, if you
wanted to scale the cluster,

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:25.610
the same thing happens.

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:28.180
It's very easy and
seamless to scale clusters,

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:30.610
because again, we're only
moving the re-assignment.

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.850
Now, remember that each node
has to have exclusive access

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:36.430
to the data that it's managing,
because we're providing

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:38.451
atonic single row transactions.

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:43.830
This means that you can
easily scale the clusters up.

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:46.500
But this also means that you can
easily scale the clusters down.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:48.750
So if you know that you need
to do some workloads that

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:52.950
will change in the size and
type and nature of the workload,

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:55.680
you can easily send
an API request,

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:59.070
resize the cluster up, do
your additional processing

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.810
with data PROC or data
flow or any other system,

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:03.000
and then scale it down.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:05.572
And Bigtable will handle the
management automatically.

00:22:08.325 --> 00:22:09.950
One interesting thing
that also happens

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:13.560
is with Bigtable, the throughput
scales linearly in proportion

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:15.240
to the number of nodes.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:18.200
For one, because it's very
easy to scale the clusters.

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:21.430
And because the nodes themselves
don't talk to each other.

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:22.920
They're completely independent.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:24.540
The throughput
scales in proportion

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:26.280
to the number of nodes.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.590
In many other storage
systems, this performance

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:30.750
would typically flatten
out after a while.

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.950
But with Bigtable,
it scales linearly.

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:37.470
So whether you have 3,
30, 300 or more nodes,

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:41.280
the throughput and processing
will scale linearly.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.740
Last year we did a test
with one of our customers

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:48.390
with 3500 Bigtable
nodes and 232 VMs,

00:22:48.390 --> 00:22:51.240
and again, the throughput
there scales linearly.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:53.040
At this point, I'll
pass it back to Leon

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:55.350
to talk about the architecture
that you saw earlier,

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.320
in more depth.

00:22:57.320 --> 00:22:58.490
LEON STEIN: Thanks, Misha.

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:07.220
So going back to our eBay
use case and our challenges--

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:12.350
so we had to synchronize a
catalog over to Google Cloud

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:16.060
platform in near real-time
with low latency--

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:19.050
we're talking about single
seconds of latency--

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.270
and with high consistency.

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:25.730
So we also want to
serve the catalog data

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:29.220
to other services
that consume it

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:33.600
on Google Cloud
Platform with high read

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:36.660
volume and low latency.

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:42.340
And we also want to support
the vision use case.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:48.000
So we want to keep listing
images in Google Cloud Platform

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:50.480
for training vision models.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:55.010
And also, we want to keep
AI vision signatures, which

00:23:55.010 --> 00:24:00.380
are feature vectors
over images somewhere,

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:07.070
so that we can extract and build
image search index out of them.

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:13.300
So we've selected Bigtable
to store catalog data, which

00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:16.010
may sound unusual.

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:18.560
It's not like Misha mentioned.

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.800
Bigtable is
unopinionated datastore.

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:25.160
So it worked really well for us.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:28.950
It is highly scalable
and reliable solution.

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:33.530
And with amount of data and
volume of updates and reads,

00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:39.140
it was a store that
could handle this.

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:41.450
It has excellent performance.

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:43.190
And another important
consideration

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:48.140
for us is the API, the
Java API, to Bigtable

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:52.290
is actually based
on HBase client.

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:55.000
So if you don't
know about HBase,

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:59.750
Apache's HBase is open
source NoSQL database

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:05.630
that was modeled after Google
published Bigtable paper.

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:11.930
So HBase was modeled after
Bigtable, and the ideas

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:15.310
that Apache Bigtable
utilizes are the ones that

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:18.230
were used by Bigtable.

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:22.090
And it's very nice
that Google chose

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:26.780
to implement [INAUDIBLE]
client as fully

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:28.260
compatible HBase client.

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.740
So you can write code
against Bigtable,

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:34.460
as you would write
it against HBase.

00:25:34.460 --> 00:25:40.070
So compared to HBase and another
very popular NoSQL database,

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:45.230
Cassandra, is the
performance of Bigtable

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:48.750
is significantly higher.

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:53.900
So in addition it is
fully managed, of course,

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.700
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:59.330
So while Cassandra
and HBased deployments

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:06.680
are nontrivial to support,
and especially at large scale,

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:12.973
it is well integrated with GCP
console and rest of the Google

00:26:12.973 --> 00:26:13.970
Cloud Platform stack.

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:18.001
As Misha mentioned, there
are connectors from Dataproc,

00:26:18.001 --> 00:26:18.500
Dataflow.

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:24.730
So-- and going back
to performance,

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:29.590
this chart shows a benchmark
that actually Google

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:34.150
made a couple of years ago,
comparing Bigtable performance

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:36.670
to HBase and Cassandra.

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:43.420
And you can see that the writes
and reads is where Bigtable

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:46.570
consistently takes six
milliseconds operation,

00:26:46.570 --> 00:26:53.140
and HBase and Cassandra are
in hundreds of milliseconds.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.650
Well, Cassandra does better on--

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:57.820
obviously on writes,
because that's

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:00.490
what it was optimized for.

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:04.960
So another great
features of Bigtable

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:07.900
is seamless cluster resizing.

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:14.230
So we run pretty large Bigtable
instance on hundreds of nodes,

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:18.990
but sometimes we need
to make bulk loads that

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:23.560
require much more resources.

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:25.480
And we can add 100's
or a few hundred

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:28.390
nodes to our Bigtable instance.

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:36.400
And in 20 minutes, it is all
re-balanced and ready to go

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:38.560
and we can send the
load to Bigtable.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:43.330
And then after we are done,
we can remove this nodes

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:45.730
and it just continues going.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:49.710
We don't see any
degradation in performance

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:50.990
as re-balancing is happening.

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:53.230
It's works really, really great.

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:57.400
Also, the administrative
operations against Bigtable,

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:00.620
like adding new tables,
changing column families,

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:03.580
they're all nonblocking
and they can done

00:28:03.580 --> 00:28:06.830
on running Bigtable instance.

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:11.980
And again, there is no effect
on running applications.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:16.360
Finally, a lot of
magic, I guess,

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:21.100
as we been working with
Bigtable for the last year,

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:25.480
we realized that a lot
of magic with performance

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:26.950
goes into the client.

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:33.050
And Google is actively working
on open source Bigtable client.

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:38.680
And we were really happy
with all the optimizations

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:42.490
that we've seen that went into
the client over the past year,

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.970
and we are sure it will
continue going this way.

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:49.770
So back to the diagram.

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:53.670
So this is the
diagram at high level.

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:57.060
You can see is that we use many
different components on GCP

00:28:57.060 --> 00:29:00.690
platform just to support
catalog ingestion.

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:04.680
So let's focus on our
near-real-time synchronization

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:06.030
pipeline.

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:10.680
So in eBay core on
the left, you see

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:17.280
we have our private cloud,
where we have Compute, Storage,

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:23.460
and Streaming, where data
is originally hosted.

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:27.300
So we have exposed
all the updates

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:31.630
that go to different data sets
that comprise eBay catalog.

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:33.990
We have exposed them
as Kafka streams.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:37.680
So there are dozens
of different datasets

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:41.760
that compose eBay listings,
which are partitioned

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:43.920
across many Kafka streams.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:49.770
So we are dealing with close
to 500 individual streams.

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:55.260
And this streams are
consumed by storm typologies

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:58.710
that we manage and
run on eBay side.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:02.220
If we probably would
have considered

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.120
Apache Beam for that.

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:10.680
But we have storm typologies
that consumes this events.

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:13.200
It aggregates them
in micro batches,

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:18.270
does duplications, and then
sends this micro batches over

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:19.920
to GCP.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:24.760
We use Google Cloud load
balancer right at France--

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:32.430
Kubernetes cluster of
our catalog service--

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:35.100
that receives this
micro batches--

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:38.040
and micro batches may
contain anywhere from 1

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:42.150
to a few hundred records--

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:45.840
and then insert
them into Bigtable.

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:53.460
So now let's look
at the table that--

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:55.290
where we store that data.

00:30:55.290 --> 00:31:00.780
So we chose to store the
data in the original format

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:04.020
that we get from core.

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:06.210
And the reason for
that is we want

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.970
to be really flexible in
how we expose this data.

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:13.770
So the most flexibility
for us would

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:16.690
be not to make any
changes to raw data,

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:23.850
but rather have a separate table
where we convert and transform

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:24.370
the data.

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:28.440
So having data in the
original format in GCP

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:32.010
gives us the ability to go
back and recompute our target

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:33.990
model at any point of time.

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:41.700
The data sets that
we got from core are

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:44.580
included in Apache avro format.

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:47.730
So every cell here
actually contains

00:31:47.730 --> 00:31:52.890
a record with multiple fields
that are encoded in avro.

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:55.180
We have a separate table
where we store avro schemas,

00:31:55.180 --> 00:31:58.270
so we don't lose track of those.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:01.920
And you can see that we have
multiple column families.

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:05.370
So in fact, for
every dataset we use,

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:07.190
we have a separate
column family.

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:17.010
And that helps us isolating
the load on Bigtable because--

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:18.770
the nice thing about
column families

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:22.000
is that if you have
independent reads--

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:27.600
so if you read or write to
a particular column family,

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:30.270
it doesn't feel as
a column families.

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:31.980
So you can have
many of them, you

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:37.455
can have hundreds of column
families, but you only--

00:32:37.455 --> 00:32:43.200
you need to writes or reads
only ones that you touch matter.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.630
So in every column family
we have two columns.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:50.850
One for meta data, that
captures information

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:54.450
on where the record originated
from, some correlation,

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:56.600
identifiers.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:58.320
Some of the streams
that we consume

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:01.200
come from Oracle
Golden Gate, so it

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:06.690
will capture Oracle row ids,
transaction commit sequences,

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:07.740
and whatnot.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:13.540
And then the avro records
that contains the data itself.

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:19.100
So you will also notice
that we have NRT,

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:22.020
the column family prefix
is NRT, which stands

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:24.240
for near-real-time, and DW.

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:27.400
So we'll cover it later.

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:29.310
For some data sets,
these are duplicated.

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:32.430
So the DW are coming from Batch.

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:36.270
And I'll explain
later why we do that.

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:39.930
Now going to row key design--

00:33:39.930 --> 00:33:42.420
as I mentioned,
listings are comprised

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:45.330
on many different
kinds of records.

00:33:45.330 --> 00:33:51.390
And you can look at it as
hierarchical parent-child data

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:52.320
structure.

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:55.050
So if you have a
listing 1, 2, 3,

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:59.490
it may have variance 1 and 2.

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:06.050
So we prefix the key
by the listing ID.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:08.850
Then we put colon
qualifier for the kind

00:34:08.850 --> 00:34:11.929
of child we are
writing, and then

00:34:11.929 --> 00:34:17.310
colon and then the
identifier for that child.

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:19.830
And then if you
have grandchildren,

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:23.840
we'll keep appending to the key.

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:26.580
So that allows us to keep
all the records belonging

00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:28.110
to the same listing
together, which

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:30.389
is really important
with Bigtable,

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:34.750
because they can be scanned
together really efficiently.

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:39.270
So Bigtable allows
scanning by key prefix

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:43.260
and this records are
physically stored together,

00:34:43.260 --> 00:34:46.650
so you can read
them really quickly.

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:49.350
Now with key design,
it is really important

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:55.110
to avoid hot spotting,
which is having

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:58.360
keys that are monotonically
increasing or decreasing.

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:01.170
So with eBay listings, we
do not have exactly that,

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:07.770
but the distribution of keys
of listing identifiers in eBay

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:09.960
is not exactly uniform.

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:12.910
So we chose to reverse
lexicographically

00:35:12.910 --> 00:35:16.380
the listing IDs.

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:19.320
And that makes
distribution uniform

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:22.890
and avoids any
potential interference

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:27.360
with how the data is
distributed in Bigtable

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:30.350
and how it is scanned.

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:36.530
And you also notice
that these gray cells

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:40.250
contain no data, which
is fine because Bigtable

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:46.200
doesn't punish you for
having sparse data sets.

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:51.185
So you don't pay, you don't--

00:35:51.185 --> 00:35:53.345
if data is not there,
it doesn't take space.

00:35:56.030 --> 00:36:06.620
So as a NoSQL database, Bigtable
supports a retrieval by keys.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:08.860
So if you have a key,
you can get the value,

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:10.220
or you can scan a key range.

00:36:10.220 --> 00:36:12.303
These are basically two
different fundamental axis

00:36:12.303 --> 00:36:13.460
patterns with Bigtable.

00:36:13.460 --> 00:36:17.240
So you cannot have secondary
indices supplied by Bigtable

00:36:17.240 --> 00:36:23.030
itself, but you can make one
for yourself as another table

00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:23.630
in Bigtable.

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:25.970
And we use that, since
we need to know--

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:30.890
we have accumulated over a year
or so that we have been running

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:33.290
on Google Cloud Platform--
we've accumulated

00:36:33.290 --> 00:36:36.830
billions of listings
and 100's of terabytes

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:38.350
of data in Bigtable.

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:40.790
However, it is really
important for us

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:45.060
to know which listings
are available right now,

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:48.650
which is about 1.1 billion.

00:36:48.650 --> 00:36:51.440
And we want to scan the
listings that are available

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:52.730
right now really quickly.

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:54.560
For example if you
want to rebuild

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:58.800
search index from scratch
or to run some reporting.

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:01.220
So for that we maintain
a separate table, which

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:04.660
is really small
or really narrow,

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:07.790
that only contains
the identifiers

00:37:07.790 --> 00:37:09.410
of the available listings.

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:11.390
So if listing
becomes unavailable,

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:15.320
we just delete a
record from Bigtable.

00:37:15.320 --> 00:37:17.550
Or if we get a new
listing that is available,

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:20.110
we just add a record here.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:24.170
And the identifiers,
the listing identifiers

00:37:24.170 --> 00:37:28.090
are prefixed here by site IDs.

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:32.270
And site IDs stands
pretty much for market

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:37.930
where item was listed,
United States, UK, and so on.

00:37:37.930 --> 00:37:41.420
This allows us to scan just for
available listings in United

00:37:41.420 --> 00:37:43.550
States, or just for
available listings for UK.

00:37:43.550 --> 00:37:49.340
So we can rebuild the index
just for the subset of markets

00:37:49.340 --> 00:37:52.460
that we want to.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:55.730
And then we have a few columns.

00:37:55.730 --> 00:38:00.640
In this case, we add seller_id
and leaf category ID,

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:03.200
in case if you want to
do additional filtering,

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:06.650
as we scan this data
without having to do lookups

00:38:06.650 --> 00:38:10.040
against all listings table.

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:15.500
Now the value of--

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:17.880
so as I mentioned,
the values that you

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:21.720
want to bring to the stack of
applications that use catalog,

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:27.380
is by representing eBay catalog
in a coherent, convenient way

00:38:27.380 --> 00:38:28.870
to other applications.

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:36.510
So we've developed schema,
or data structure that

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:40.230
represents eBay catalog,
which is much more convenient

00:38:40.230 --> 00:38:45.360
than the raw data that we get.

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:48.440
So we store the data
in a separate table

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:56.490
and we chose protobuffers
to represent that schema.

00:38:56.490 --> 00:39:00.780
And we also break
it up into fragments

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:05.160
that we store across
column families

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:08.810
so that we can retrieve them
separately for efficiency.

00:39:08.810 --> 00:39:12.780
So in case if you only need
to know about variance,

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:16.890
you can just scan
that column family.

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:23.520
We use-- here, each record
contains a single listing ID--

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:25.240
each record contains
single listing,

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:28.280
so the keys are
lexicographically

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:30.600
reversed listing IDs.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:35.550
And we also have this
src_version column

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:41.130
that we use for lazy
rebuilding catalog model.

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:46.980
So usually we rebuild catalog
model as we ingest new data.

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:50.700
But in some cases when
we do batch operation,

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:57.270
we can just implement
the version in this table

00:39:57.270 --> 00:39:59.190
without rebuilding
the whole model.

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:01.470
So next time we need
to read, we knows

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:08.550
that the model that we have
in this table is stale,

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:12.390
and we need to rebuild it.

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:18.760
So next, the Catalog service.

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:21.990
So Catalog service is running--

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:28.160
or as I mentioned on
Kubernetes on Container Engine,

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:33.980
we've chosen to front all access
to Bigtable by this service.

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:38.060
So that clients that need
to deal with Catalog data,

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:40.430
they have to go through
Catalog service.

00:40:40.430 --> 00:40:42.500
They don't have to
deal with intricacies

00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:47.380
of Bigtable storage.

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:49.730
And this service,
you can imagine,

00:40:49.730 --> 00:40:53.510
it runs on pretty large
number of instances

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:58.280
because it gets a lot of read
and write hits from all over.

00:40:58.280 --> 00:41:03.410
And Container Engine
really helps us

00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:09.410
with managing deployments and
scaling of our Catalog service.

00:41:09.410 --> 00:41:11.930
We have it fully integrated
in continuous delivery

00:41:11.930 --> 00:41:14.840
pipeline that we run on Core.

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:19.195
And we write our images to
Container Registry in Google

00:41:19.195 --> 00:41:24.110
Cloud Platform and do
rollouts in managed way,

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:27.860
so it doesn't impact the
capacity of our service.

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:31.010
So it's been working
really, really well.

00:41:31.010 --> 00:41:37.010
So this service is
processing many thousands

00:41:37.010 --> 00:41:41.630
of batches per second,
with every batch containing

00:41:41.630 --> 00:41:45.335
hundreds of records, so it's
pretty heavy volume service.

00:41:49.420 --> 00:41:51.780
Now I've mentioned that
we needed the mechanism

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:54.535
to ensure that we
don't miss updates.

00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:00.930
Our near-real-time pipeline is
designed not to miss updates.

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:04.530
So if for some reason we
cannot write to Bigtable,

00:42:04.530 --> 00:42:09.860
we'll just go into this infinite
retrial loop, and then alert,

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:14.220
and then someone has to come
in and fix the pipeline so that

00:42:14.220 --> 00:42:15.690
updates keep flowing.

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:19.590
But it happens that upstream
we might miss an event

00:42:19.590 --> 00:42:22.630
just because something
happened up stream from us.

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:26.070
So for that-- and also
to support datasets

00:42:26.070 --> 00:42:29.500
that are not available
in near-real-time,

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:32.610
we've added a Batch pipeline.

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:35.520
So Batch pipeline
synchronizes some

00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:39.690
of the main datasets about
the eBay catalog that

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:43.730
contain price and
availability information,

00:42:43.730 --> 00:42:45.420
and we do it daily.

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:48.000
So in case if we missed
price or availability update

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:51.060
at the end of the day, we'll
catch it with a batch update,

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:52.560
and reconcile it.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:56.370
And so this inconsistency,
we'll leave in our catalog

00:42:56.370 --> 00:42:57.490
for up to a day.

00:42:57.490 --> 00:43:02.250
We also have a dashboard
for highly visible items.

00:43:02.250 --> 00:43:10.020
We can compare what is in Core
systems to what is in Bigtable,

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:13.350
and then force synchronization.

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:16.800
Now the way Batch
pipeline works--

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:20.880
first, we have--
of course much data

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:25.230
is extracted from our Hadoop
and Teradata data warehouses

00:43:25.230 --> 00:43:27.690
that run in Core.

00:43:27.690 --> 00:43:31.860
And then this extracts
that rerun data

00:43:31.860 --> 00:43:36.710
broken into many small fragments
containing up to a few million

00:43:36.710 --> 00:43:37.860
of records.

00:43:37.860 --> 00:43:43.770
And we load this batches
over to Google Cloud Platform

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:46.200
and we put them in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:50.700
And after each fragment
is transferred,

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:54.780
we invoke a call
to Catalog service

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:57.270
so that it can load it
from Google Cloud Storage

00:43:57.270 --> 00:44:01.350
and stream it from Google
Cloud Storage and stream

00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:04.770
this batch into Bigtable.

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:06.250
This operation is
very important.

00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:10.320
So if something breaks, we
can repeat until we succeed.

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:12.540
And we keep track of
all the fragments,

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:17.160
both on Core, in a
simple SQL database so we

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:18.930
can account for each fragment.

00:44:18.930 --> 00:44:22.980
So we make sure that everything
is loaded completely.

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:26.760
And we also keep track
of this batch operations

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:31.710
on Bigtable side, so that our
load operation is effectively

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:32.340
nonblocking.

00:44:32.340 --> 00:44:34.830
So we just make a
call to load and then

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:38.400
we pull against HTTP
API of Catalog service

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:40.500
to check whether the
fragment is loaded,

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:43.575
which may take from a few
seconds to couple of minutes.

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:53.370
So next part is
integration with Search.

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:56.130
So Search is really
the main consumer

00:44:56.130 --> 00:44:59.130
of catalog data
for us, so it has

00:44:59.130 --> 00:45:04.260
to reindex listings in real time
as listing information changes.

00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:09.630
In the retail world, you
have to index in real time.

00:45:09.630 --> 00:45:12.870
We've chosen to put Pub/Sub
between our search index

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:16.440
build and our
catalog service, just

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:19.700
to give a little
room for our index

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:24.210
build process to
adjust to spikes

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:29.970
in the number of updates.

00:45:29.970 --> 00:45:35.500
So if there is a big spike,
Pub/Sub will buffer it,

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:38.880
and Search can work
at its own pace.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:41.590
Search indexing can
work at its own pace.

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:51.400
The way it works, as data is
pushed to Catalog service,

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:54.340
is the identifiers of the
listings that are affected,

00:45:54.340 --> 00:45:56.440
along with some meta
data that tells which

00:45:56.440 --> 00:46:01.240
dataset this update is coming
from, and actually in a bitmap

00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:04.830
containing the
indices of columns

00:46:04.830 --> 00:46:09.610
that got touched by that
update, is put on Pub/Sub.

00:46:09.610 --> 00:46:11.980
So each message may
contain a number

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:14.520
of listings corresponding
to the micro batch,

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:15.880
along with the meta data.

00:46:15.880 --> 00:46:20.110
So search index build
consumes this messages

00:46:20.110 --> 00:46:21.790
from Pub/Sub topic.

00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:25.960
And based on what
changed about listings,

00:46:25.960 --> 00:46:31.410
it decides what it needs to
fetch from Catalog service.

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:35.020
It usually needs to fetch more
than just the dataset that

00:46:35.020 --> 00:46:37.960
changed, because
indexing involves

00:46:37.960 --> 00:46:40.960
pretty complex
operations that requires

00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:43.270
knowing a lot about listing.

00:46:43.270 --> 00:46:48.040
But in certain cases,
it can just take--

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:50.590
it just needs to know a
subset of listing data,

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:52.010
and really helps
us with reducing

00:46:52.010 --> 00:46:56.260
the load against Catalog
Service and Bigtable.

00:46:56.260 --> 00:47:03.760
So that is pretty much how our
near-real-time search indexing

00:47:03.760 --> 00:47:06.100
works.

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:14.120
So next, as I mentioned, our
ShopBot supports image search.

00:47:14.120 --> 00:47:20.030
You snap a picture, or
use an existing picture.

00:47:20.030 --> 00:47:23.270
You give it to
ShopBot, and it finds

00:47:23.270 --> 00:47:25.880
items that are available
that looks similarly.

00:47:25.880 --> 00:47:27.500
It works really well.

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:30.590
It is a image
search that we have.

00:47:30.590 --> 00:47:34.010
The image models are
developed in-house.

00:47:34.010 --> 00:47:36.070
In fact, we've just
submitted-- a couple

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:41.120
of weeks ago-- submitted
a paper to KDD 2017

00:47:41.120 --> 00:47:45.920
where we describe in detail
how we build our image models

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:52.080
and how our pipelines and
image search works, in detail.

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:54.410
So if you're interested,
please ping me

00:47:54.410 --> 00:47:58.050
offline and I can give you
more information about that.

00:47:58.050 --> 00:48:00.445
So our Catalog
service in Bigtable

00:48:00.445 --> 00:48:03.950
will ultimately
participate in the process

00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:08.790
of helping enable image search.

00:48:08.790 --> 00:48:13.250
So what happens is, as
we get catalog data,

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:20.690
we detect if we have new images,
or list of images for a listing

00:48:20.690 --> 00:48:21.530
changed.

00:48:21.530 --> 00:48:26.360
So in response, we download
images from eBay Core.

00:48:26.360 --> 00:48:31.310
We save the images in GCS,
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:48:31.310 --> 00:48:36.470
And we also have GPU cluster
where we're on our own vision

00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:43.250
models, where we pass the images
to compute feature vectors

00:48:43.250 --> 00:48:45.260
representing these images.

00:48:45.260 --> 00:48:47.480
And storing these
feature vectors

00:48:47.480 --> 00:48:51.680
which are half a megabyte--

00:48:54.500 --> 00:49:05.240
512 bytes-- half a Kilobyte
records to Bigtable.

00:49:05.240 --> 00:49:11.130
So here is how our
images table looks like.

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:15.890
So it has image unique
identifier as a key.

00:49:15.890 --> 00:49:22.610
And it stores a couple of
versions of image signatures.

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:24.830
The reason we store
a couple of versions,

00:49:24.830 --> 00:49:28.170
it helps us transitioning from
one vision model to another.

00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:33.110
So in case, it helps with
smooth transitioning of that.

00:49:33.110 --> 00:49:35.540
Finally we have
another small table

00:49:35.540 --> 00:49:42.100
that just keeps image hashes
over the bits of images.

00:49:42.100 --> 00:49:44.030
And that helps us the
duplicating the images.

00:49:44.030 --> 00:49:46.670
In our business, we find
that quite a lot, about 20%

00:49:46.670 --> 00:49:48.890
of images, that
actually duplicates

00:49:48.890 --> 00:49:50.930
and they are submitted
under different names.

00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:54.320
So this helps us identify if
you already have seen and image

00:49:54.320 --> 00:49:57.451
in the past, in that case we
don't save it in Google Cloud

00:49:57.451 --> 00:49:57.950
Storage.

00:49:57.950 --> 00:50:01.550
It helps us with reducing
our Google Cloud Storage

00:50:01.550 --> 00:50:05.050
requirements.

00:50:05.050 --> 00:50:11.700
Finally, we do batch
processing against Bigtable.

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:14.640
So this batch processing--
we have several use cases

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:16.350
for batch processing.

00:50:16.350 --> 00:50:21.570
One of them is
building image index.

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:25.650
And for that, we need to
scan all available listings,

00:50:25.650 --> 00:50:30.110
and we use that
available listings table

00:50:30.110 --> 00:50:34.290
that I mentioned that we have,
and then for this listings

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:36.060
we retrieve all the
image signatures

00:50:36.060 --> 00:50:40.110
and write them to GCS,
Google Cloud Storage,

00:50:40.110 --> 00:50:45.720
and that partitioned
by category.

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:47.850
And then it gets
distributed to a cluster

00:50:47.850 --> 00:50:50.520
that serves image searches.

00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:55.250
So we use both Cloud
Dataproc and Cloud

00:50:55.250 --> 00:50:59.030
Dataflow to scan Bigtable.

00:50:59.030 --> 00:51:02.310
It is great support for that.

00:51:02.310 --> 00:51:05.700
So just if you've--

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:09.960
best practices and
tricks about Bigtable.

00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:12.900
So first, pre-join your data.

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:17.160
If you have many datasets
that share the same key,

00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:19.950
it's best to put them
in the same table

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:21.330
and then you can leverage--

00:51:21.330 --> 00:51:25.800
and then you scan and
access data together.

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:30.440
You can also leverage atomicity
that transaction support

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:33.510
at row level that Bigtable has.

00:51:33.510 --> 00:51:41.460
And then, column families
allow you to separate the load

00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:43.800
with different access patterns.

00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:47.520
And And then with key design--

00:51:47.520 --> 00:51:51.020
First, you need to design a
key to avoid hot spotting,

00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:52.500
and we covered
that, and there are

00:51:52.500 --> 00:51:54.810
several methods of doing that.

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:57.546
You can use hash of the key.

00:51:57.546 --> 00:51:58.920
You can choose
one of the columns

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:00.960
in the dataset as a prefix.

00:52:00.960 --> 00:52:03.390
Or like we did, you
can try to reversing

00:52:03.390 --> 00:52:05.340
your key lexicographically,
and that may

00:52:05.340 --> 00:52:06.990
improve your key distribution.

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:14.970
And also key prefixes help
with optimizing queries,

00:52:14.970 --> 00:52:19.800
like we do when we want to scan
listings for specific markets.

00:52:19.800 --> 00:52:22.620
And also, you can
use key prefixes

00:52:22.620 --> 00:52:25.710
to represent parent-child
relationships.

00:52:25.710 --> 00:52:30.380
So finally, we use
Monitoring with Stackdriver.

00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:35.070
And that helps us
with this 500 streams

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:38.100
that we need to
babysit and manage.

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:42.170
It helps us keep track of those.

00:52:42.170 --> 00:52:46.740
We have custom time series
that we've designed.

00:52:46.740 --> 00:52:51.870
And we post our latencies
between when event happened

00:52:51.870 --> 00:52:54.520
on eBay Core-side, and
when the record got

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:58.200
inserted in to Bigtable,
and also the volumes.

00:52:58.200 --> 00:53:01.590
And this is a snapshot of one
of our Stackdriver dashboards

00:53:01.590 --> 00:53:05.230
that shows the
volume of the data.

00:53:05.230 --> 00:53:07.740
So you can see-- you probably
cannot see the numbers,

00:53:07.740 --> 00:53:13.980
but it has all 500 datasets
here, in one place.

00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:21.150
And the top one shows a number
of batches and this lines

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:23.460
that are kind of on the top--

00:53:23.460 --> 00:53:29.650
it's about 20,000
every five minutes.

00:53:29.650 --> 00:53:32.580
So it's a lot.

00:53:32.580 --> 00:53:37.830
We have alerts set
of this monitoring.

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:42.270
So in case of things are
not moving fast enough,

00:53:42.270 --> 00:53:45.510
we get an alert and we can get
in and add nodes to Bigtable

00:53:45.510 --> 00:53:46.350
or fix things.

00:53:46.350 --> 00:53:51.486
With that I pass it back to
Misha for some closing notes.

00:53:51.486 --> 00:53:56.944
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:56.944 --> 00:53:59.110
MISHA BRUKMAN: So I'd like
to thank very much, Leon.

00:53:59.110 --> 00:54:02.650
And we have the folks from eBay,
the team that worked on this,

00:54:02.650 --> 00:54:05.420
and also we have some folks
from the Bigtable team.

00:54:05.420 --> 00:54:07.830
So thank you very much
everyone for contributing

00:54:07.830 --> 00:54:09.070
to this project.

00:54:09.070 --> 00:54:11.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

